Subject: Re-assert NetworkManager's statistics refresh rate only when it is not 500 ms

RefreshRateMs is one property of the NetworkManager device, shared by
every client, and each session with a network sensor runs its own
ksystemstats. The refreshRateMsChanged handler took every other change
signal for a foreign write and set the rate again, so with two sessions
logged in each instance answered the other's writes without end: an NM
audit line per write, about 130 a second, and some 40% of a CPU spent in
NetworkManager and the system bus. The handler tests the value instead:
it records a rate other than UpdateRate as the one to restore on exit and
sets UpdateRate again, and a rate already at UpdateRate, whichever
instance wrote it, ends the exchange.

---
--- a/plugins/network/NetworkManagerBackend.cpp
+++ b/plugins/network/NetworkManagerBackend.cpp
@@ -54,15 +54,14 @@
     // the oustide rate. override any changes and restore it when we are destroyed.
     m_initialStatisticsRate = m_statistics->refreshRateMs();
     connect(m_statistics.get(), &NetworkManager::DeviceStatistics::refreshRateMsChanged, this, [this] (uint rate) {
-        // Unfortunately we always get a change signal even when disconnecting before the setter and
-        // connecting afterwards, so we have to do this and assume the first signal after a call is
-        // caused by it. Iniitally true because of the call below
-        static bool updatingRefreshRate = true;
-        if (!updatingRefreshRate) {
+        // Every client of the device sees every write, its own and those of other
+        // ksystemstats instances (one per session). Re-assert only a rate that differs
+        // from ours: answering a write of UpdateRate would make two instances answer
+        // each other without end.
+        if (rate != UpdateRate) {
             m_initialStatisticsRate = rate;
             m_statistics->setRefreshRateMs(UpdateRate);
         }
-        updatingRefreshRate = !updatingRefreshRate;
     });
 
     // We want to display speed in bytes per second, so use a fixed one-second
